  "title": "Kourovka problem 14.44 (main)",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "Let k(X) denote the number of conjugacy classes of a finite group X. Suppose\nthat a finite group G = AB is a product of two subgroups A, B of coprime orders. Is\nit true that k(AB) ⩽ k(A)k(B)?\n    Note that one cannot drop the coprimeness condition and the answer is positive\nif one of the subgroups is normal, see Archive, 11.43.   L. S. Kazarin, J. Sangroniz",
